GT_S3 = """For decades, local libraries were judged mainly by how many books they lent. Today many librarians describe their role as supporting “information confidence” — helping people judge sources, complete online forms, and access public services that have moved behind digital doors. In towns where bank branches and council desks have closed, the library often becomes the last free indoor place with wifi, printers and staff who will sit with a confused user for twenty minutes.

This shift is uneven. Well-funded city systems run coding clubs and maker spaces; rural branches may open only three afternoons a week and rely on volunteers. National statistics that celebrate rising digital session counts can hide the fact that print borrowing still matters most for children under eight and for readers who cannot afford devices. Campaigners therefore urge funders to measure outcomes such as successful benefit applications assisted, not only wifi hours logged.

Staff training has not always kept up. A librarian hired to recommend novels may now be asked to explain a housing portal that changes its password rules every quarter. Some systems hire digital-help assistants on short contracts; others simply add the work to the existing desk. Burnout shows up as shorter opening hours, which then look, in the next budget, like proof that demand is falling.

Critics worry that mission creep turns libraries into unofficial social-work offices without training or safeguarding resources. Supporters reply that refusing help with a housing form is itself a choice — one that pushes vulnerable residents toward paid intermediaries. The practical middle path emerging in several regions is partnership: librarians handle navigation and appointments; specialist charities take complex cases. Where that partnership is funded, doorstep evictions linked to missed online forms decline. Where it is not, the wifi statistics still look healthy, and the people who needed a person never appear in the count.

Print collections remain the quiet majority of what children actually take home. A tablet club that photographs well does not replace the stack of picture books by the door. The writer’s argument is that libraries are being asked to be two institutions at once, and that only one of those institutions is cheap to count. If funders want the civic one — the twenty minutes with a confused user — they have to budget for staff time, not only for broadband."""

