GT_S6 = """Night markets are often described as a tourist spectacle, but for many cities they are first a labour market. Stallholders rent a few square metres, bring a generator or tap a shared cable, and sell food that would not survive a high-street lease. The same markets also organise social time: families eat standing up after late shifts, and students revise under the same lights that advertise noodles. When a council treats the market only as a brand, it tends to raise pitch fees, standardise stalls, and push out the vendors who cannot afford a matching awning.

Hygiene rules are not the enemy of this economy, but they are often written for kitchens with sinks and staff rotas. A cart that moves each night cannot plumb a grease trap to the standard of a restaurant. Cities that want both safety and survival invent intermediate licences: sealed water tanks, inspected once a month, instead of a full commercial kitchen. Where that licence does not exist, enforcement arrives as a raid, and the market empties for a week before returning two streets away.

Residents complain about noise, smoke and parking. Those complaints are sometimes a proxy for who is seen to belong on the pavement after dark. A market that stays open until 02:00 on a street of new apartments will lose the political fight unless hours, extraction fans and a complaints number are negotiated before the first stall opens. Some cities now publish a one-page compact: hours, waste collection time, and a named officer. It is dull paperwork, and it is what keeps the lights on.

The writer does not romanticise every stall. Overcrowding, child labour and counterfeit goods appear where inspections are for show. The claim is that night markets are infrastructure, like a bus route: they move food, wages and people through hours when the formal city is closed. Pricing them as a festival, then being surprised when vendors cannot pay festival rents, is a policy error dressed as culture."""
